  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Reply A is:\n```yaml\n{\"first\": 1}\n```\n{\"second\": 2}\nReply B is:\n{\"second\": 2}\nThe rule requires Reply A to be scored as {\"first\": 1}, because it contains a code fence holding an object, and the last object inside a code fence is {\"first\": 1}. Reply B has no code fence, so the rule requires it to be scored as the last top-level object, {\"second\": 2}. The check, however, returns {\"second\": 2} for both replies, because its fence regex only recognizes ```json, ```JSON, or an unlabeled fence, not ```yaml, so it treats the fenced object in Reply A as a top-level object and picks the later one. I would be wrong if the check returned {\"first\": 1} for Reply A, or if either reply raised."
